Foreword 
 
This report presents detailed tabulations of data from the 2000 FSM Census of Population and Housing on the 
population’s demographic, social, and economic characteristics.  Summary tabulations, including housing 
characteristics are presented in the 2000 FSM National Census Report.  This report contains text (this foreword 
and two appendices), a table of contents followed by maps (one combined for the nation, and one for each of 
the four states), the detailed tables, and a copy of the questionnaire (appendix C). 
 
The first set of tables (Tables 1-1 to 1-19) provides the distribution of the population classified by age, sex, 
ethnicity, place of birth, legal residence and citizenship, educational attainment, labor force participation, work 
status, industry, occupation, and income.  In subsequent series, these and other basic characteristics such as 
language, household size, fertility, marital status, religion, length of continuous residence, literacy, vocational 
training, veteran status, economic activities and place of work, etc., are cross-classified by geographic, 
demographic, social, and economic measures as follows: 
 
• Tables 2-1 to 2-19, Tabulations by Place of Usual Residence 
• Tables 3-1 to 3-17, Tabulations by Age  
• Tables 4-1 to 4-18, Tabulations by Ethnicity 
• Tables 5-1 to 5-18, Tabulations by Place of Birth 
• Tables 6-1 to 6-18, Tabulations by Place Legal Residence/Citizenship 
• Tables 7-1 to 7-18, Tabulations by Educational Attainment 
• Tables 8-1 to 8-16, Tabulations by Occupation 
• Tables 9-1 to 9-14, Tabulations by Labor Force Participation 
• Tables 10-1 to 10-16, Tabulations by Industry 
• Tables 11-1 to 11-18, Tabulations by Income 
 
Each table is uniquely identified by a table number and title.  The stubhead at the left under the title defines the 
types of geographic, demographic, or economic category for which data are shown in the particular table, and is 
part of the table title. 
 
 Appendix A provides definitions and explanations for the subject covered in this report.  Appendix B explains 
the residence rules used during the enumeration and describes the data collection and processing procedures.  
Appendix C contains the 2000 FSM Population and Housing Census Questionnaire. 
 
The content and procedures of the 1994 and 2000 FSM Census were determined based on the 1990 Census of 
Palau, Guam, American Samoa, and the Northern Mariana Islands, modified to fit the situation in the Federated 
States of Micronesia.  Further information on comparability appears in Appendix A, Definitions and 
Explanations. 
 
More detailed information on the technical and procedural matters are also described in separate analytical 
reports of the 2000 FSM Census – that is, the FSM National Census Report, available at the Division of 
Statistics, Department of Economic Affairs, P.O. Box PS-12, Palikir, Pohnpei, FM 96941. The office can be 
reached by email at census@mail.fm.    
 
x  Division of Statistics, Department of Economic Affairs 
Each State also produced a similar report with tables focusing on the individual state.  The state reports and 
tables provide a breakdown on data by municipality. 
 
Derived measures 
 
This report presents medians, means, and percentages, as well as certain rates and ratios. The median – a type of 
average – is the middle value in a distribution; i.e., the median divides the distribution into two equal parts, with 
one-half of all cases falling below the median and one-half exceeding the median.  The median is computed on 
the basis of the distribution as tabulated, which sometimes more detailed than the distribution shown in this 
report. 
 
Means are used along with medians, for tabulations on income.  The mean income is the sum of all income in a 
region or characteristic divided by the number of income recipients.  Similarly, the aggregate of all incomes in a 
region can be found by multiplying the mean of the number of persons earning income. 
 
Percents and other derived  measures which round to less than 0.1 are not shown but indicated as zero (in this 
case, that is, “-“).  
 
Symbols 
 
The following symbols are used in the tables: 
 
A dash “-“ represents or a percent which rounds to less than 0.1. 
 
Three dots “…” means not applicable, or information suppressed for reasons of confidentiality. 
 
NA means not available. 
 
